## Room pages

Use the room name as the heading. Group lighting, climate, media and other functions together, and keep the names people gave their devices. Show useful room context, such as a temperature with its unit and its state.

Use the same control pattern in every room. A lamp must not need a tap in one room and a hidden long press in another without a real reason.

## Images and icons

A room image helps people recognise the room, and a device icon helps them scan. Both support the room and device names. They do not replace them. Keep sensor values and controls off busy images.

## Responsive and shared use

Allow scrolling and a single column. A wall-mounted display and a phone can arrange cards differently, but they must use the same labels, states and control meanings.

## Decide whether the task needs a data dashboard

Use a monitoring overview for a question people return to, with data you can keep current. Give occasional readers enough context to understand each measure. A fixed report suits a one-off explanation or a result that needs interpretation. Review the choice when the household, the sources or the task changes.

| Household need | Suitable presentation | Essential context |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Check grid import repeatedly during a chosen period | A few indicators, an interval chart and its values | Period, unit, source, missing intervals, and whether a sum is partial or complete |
| Compare a few room observations at a stated time | A text-first report or table | Observation time for each room, source, coverage and why an old value is excluded |
| Explain a change or investigate a fault | A focused history view or written report | Evidence, comparison conditions and an explanation the overview cannot give |

The [data guidance](../styles/brand-data.html) links these choices to charts, dashboards and maps. **BG-DAT-01** starts with the question, **BG-DAT-03** supplies the measure, period and source, and **BG-DAT-07** gives a usable alternative. [BG-COL-02](../styles/colour-web-app.html#bg-col-02) keeps identity, status and data colours apart. [BG-COL-07](../styles/chart-colour.html#bg-col-07) keeps chart identities and scales meaningful.

## A monitoring overview with matching chart data

This example answers one question about grid import. The indicators, the column chart and the table use the same six hourly intervals. One interval is missing, so the page says the total is a partial sum and qualifies the largest recorded value. A local link explains the source and coverage. It does not open a pretend device control.

The chart is a static SVG with opaque square columns, a zero baseline and the shared grid-import colour. It does not run the native Energy adapter. For the production integration and its scope, see [Charts and tables](../components/charts.html). All values are invented.

