## A text-first report when coverage is incomplete

This example keeps every room visible. It separates readings inside the review window, an older reading and a room with no configured sensor. It uses a reading list because the task does not need a chart. The source details explain why there is no household average.

The five-minute window is a choice made for this example. A real installation needs a freshness rule for each source. A theme cannot work out freshness from colour or a generic last-changed time. See [Entity state, confidence and freshness](../home-assistant/entity-state.html).

<%= example('dashboard-data-room-report') %>

## Keep native ownership

Home Assistant keeps control of selected periods, statistics, permissions and device capabilities. These layouts are reference examples, not dashboard configuration or a native adapter. A household can use its own name and identity without changing what a chart source, a warning or a missing reading means.

Before you connect a similar layout to real data, check that indicator values, plotted points and table rows agree. Repeat the check when the period or source changes. Show missing intervals (WG-076), keep power and energy apart (WG-070), and check scrolling at narrow width, keyboard access, text scaling and the reading order without the chart. Record these checks separately from source review and from a documentation build.

## Test the task, including failure

Start from the current confirmed state. Reach every action with the keyboard and check that its label names the object it affects. Check pending, failed and unavailable results, and do not replace missing data with zero. Repeat at narrow width and with enlarged text. A documentation example shows presentation only. The connected application must separately prove permissions, native routing, state confirmation and recovery.
